    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-i-of-louvain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/godfrey-i-of-louvain.jpg" alt="Godfrey I of Louvain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-i-of-louvain">Godfrey I of Louvain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godffroy1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christophre Butkens,Abraham van Diepenbeeck,Mattheus Borrekens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Brabant grew out of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>, a lordship on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyle">Dyle</a> river held from the eleventh century by a line of counts who also controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. In 1106 Emperor Henry V gave Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-i-of-louvain">Godfrey I of Louvain</a> the title of duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Lower Lorraine</a>, an old duchy that had once covered most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> but had by then dissolved into a mass of counties and bishoprics. Godfrey's successors contested this largely empty title with the rival house of Limburg. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry I</a>, associated with his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-iii-count-of-louvain">Godfrey III</a> in government from about 1183 and sole ruler from 1190, began to style himself duke of Brabant, after the name of an old district west of Brussels. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-schwabisch-hall">Diet of Schwäbisch Hall</a> in 1190 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Henry VI</a> confirmed that the ducal rank applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s own territories, which included Louvain, Brussels, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and lands stretching north towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, rather than to the whole of Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. This marked the birth of the Duchy of Brabant as a territorial principality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Henry I (d. 1235) spent the next half century enlarging it, founding towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-hertogenbosch">'s-Hertogenbosch</a> around 1185, competing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, Holland and the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Liège, and building a network of castles and chartered towns that gave the duchy its distinctively urban character.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/leuven"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leuven.jpg" alt="Leuven" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tafelrond_Leuven_-_385956_-_onroerenderfgoed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vandevorst, Kris, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Brabant was one of the most urbanised regions of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its four chief towns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-hertogenbosch">'s-Hertogenbosch</a>, along with smaller centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tienen">Tienen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nivelles">Nivelles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lier-belgium">Lier</a>, grew rich on woollen cloth woven from English wool, and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and patrician families became both partners and rivals of the dukes. When cloth-making declined in the late fourteenth century the towns turned to luxury trades, and Brussels became famous for tapestries, carved altarpieces and goldsmiths' work. The duchy's commercial centre of gravity then shifted to Antwerp. Its fairs, alongside those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen-op-zoom">Bergen op Zoom</a>, attracted English cloth merchants, South German financiers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fuggers</a> and, from about 1500, the Portuguese spice trade, and in 1531 the city opened a purpose-built exchange. By the 1560s Antwerp had around 100,000 inhabitants and was the commercial and financial capital of northern Europe, and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christophe-plantin">Christophe Plantin</a> ran one of the largest presses of the age. Most Brabanters spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloon-brabant">Walloon Brabant</a> around Nivelles spoke French. Religious life was shaped by abbeys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villers-abbey">Villers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/affligem-abbey">Affligem</a> and by mystics including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadewijch">Hadewijch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-ruusbroec">Jan van Ruusbroec</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-university-of-leuven">University of Leuven</a>, founded in 1425, became one of the leading universities of Catholic Europe. In the sixteenth century Protestant ideas spread quickly in Antwerp, preparing the conflicts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-steppes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-steppes.jpg" alt="Battle of Steppes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-steppes">Battle of Steppes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stepsheuvel_16-04-2008_13-34-59.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paul Hermans, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Brabant's dukes fought chiefly for control of the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry I</a>'s expansion towards Liège ended in defeat by the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-steppes">Battle of Steppes</a> in 1213. The duchy's greatest military success came under John I, who in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-limburg-succession">War of the Limburg Succession</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegfried-of-westerburg">Siegfried of Westerburg</a>, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, and Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-i-count-of-guelders">Reinald I of Guelders</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Battle of Worringen</a> on 5 June 1288, and so acquired the Duchy of Limburg and control of the trade route from the Rhine to the sea. The death of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-duke-of-cleves">John III</a> in 1355 without a male heir provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-brabantine-succession">War of the Brabantine Succession</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders">Louis of Male</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, defeated the Brabanters at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-scheut">Battle of Scheut</a> in August 1356 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, until a small band under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/everard-tserclaes">Everard t'Serclaes</a> recaptured the city that October. In 1371 Duke Wenceslaus was defeated and captured by the duke of Jülich at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baesweiler">Battle of Baesweiler</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> Brabant became a main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the Eighty Years' War, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-antwerp-1576">Spanish Fury at Antwerp</a> in 1576, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-farnese-duke-of-parma">Alexander Farnese</a>'s siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1584–1585, and in 1706 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Duke of Marlborough</a>'s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies">Battle of Ramillies</a> gave the allies control of the duchy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charter-of-kortenberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charter-of-kortenberg.jpg" alt="Charter of Kortenberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charter-of-kortenberg">Charter of Kortenberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charter_van_Kortenberg.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dukes governed a duchy in which the towns held unusual power. Ducal finances depended on the urban economy, and recurring debts forced rulers into concessions. In 1312 the dying John II granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-of-kortenberg">Charter of Kortenberg</a>, which created a council of four knights and ten town representatives to oversee government and allowed resistance if the duke broke its terms. The most famous of these agreements was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356">Joyous Entry</a> (<em>Blijde Inkomst</em>), sworn in January 1356 by Duchess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-duchess-of-brabant">Joanna</a> and her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-luxembourg">Wenceslaus of Luxembourg</a> as the price of their recognition. It guaranteed the duchy's indivisibility, reserved offices to natives of Brabant, required the consent of the estates for wars, coinage and new taxes, and released subjects from obedience if the ruler violated it. Every later duke, Burgundian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rulers included, swore a Joyous Entry on accession. The estates of Brabant, made up of the prelates of the great abbeys, the nobles and the chief towns, voted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> and became a permanent institution. Under the Burgundian dukes a professional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-brabant">Council of Brabant</a>, headed by a chancellor, became the duchy's high court and administrative council, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, with its palace on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coudenberg-palace">Coudenberg</a>, became the usual residence of the rulers and later of the governors-general of the Habsburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. It was in Brussels that Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> abdicated in 1555 in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356.jpg" alt="Joyous Entry of 1356" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356">Joyous Entry of 1356</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blijde_Inkomst.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nl:Johanna van Brabant en Wenceslas., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Brabant's influence was political, economic and artistic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356">Joyous Entry</a> became one of the best-known charters of liberties in European history: it was invoked against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-abjuration">Act of Abjuration</a> of 1581, by which the rebel provinces renounced Philip, is often seen as drawing on its principle that a prince who broke his oath forfeited obedience. Its memory revived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brabant-revolution">Brabant Revolution</a> of 1789. Sixteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> was the first great hub of a world economy linking the Baltic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the Iberian empires, and its commercial techniques passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> after 1585 along with many of its merchants and craftsmen. Brabant's towns sustained much of the art of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogier-van-der-weyden">Rogier van der Weyden</a> was city painter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dieric-bouts">Dieric Bouts</a> worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieter-bruegel-the-elder">Pieter Bruegel the Elder</a> lived in Antwerp and Brussels, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens">Peter Paul Rubens</a> made Antwerp the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flemish-baroque-painting">Flemish Baroque painting</a> in the seventeenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-university-of-leuven">University of Leuven</a> trained generations of Catholic scholars, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> helped to establish its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegium-trilingue">College of the Three Languages</a> in 1517. Brabant's name survives in the Belgian provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flemish-brabant">Flemish Brabant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloon-brabant">Walloon Brabant</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a>, the Brussels region lies at the heart of the old duchy, and the Belgian national anthem is called the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-brabanconne">Brabançonne</a></em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt.jpg" alt="Battle of Agincourt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Battle of Agincourt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_von_Azincourt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Duchess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-duchess-of-brabant">Joanna</a>, childless, passed the duchy to her great-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoine-duke-of-brabant">Antoine of Burgundy</a>, a younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, who was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Battle of Agincourt</a> in 1415; after his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">John IV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-saint-pol">Philip of Saint-Pol</a> died without heirs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good of Burgundy</a> inherited Brabant in 1430. From then on it was one of the Burgundian and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, keeping its estates and charters but sharing its ruler with the other provinces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> split it. Brabant joined the rising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 1570s, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-farnese-duke-of-parma">Alexander Farnese</a> reconquered the south and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1585, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> closed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> and Antwerp's trade withered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-munster">Peace of Münster</a> in 1648 confirmed the division: the north, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-hertogenbosch">'s-Hertogenbosch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-brabant">States Brabant</a>, a territory ruled directly by the States General without a voice of its own, while the south remained under Spain and from 1714 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Joseph II</a> tried to override the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356">Joyous Entry</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brabant-revolution">Brabant Revolution</a> of 1789 drove out the Austrians and proclaimed the United Belgian States in January 1790, but Austrian troops returned by the end of that year. After French victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jemappes">Jemappes</a> in 1792 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fleurus-1794">Fleurus</a> in 1794, the French Republic annexed the southern Netherlands on 1 October 1795 and divided Brabant into French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, ending the duchy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of Brabant joined a powerful urban economy to a strong tradition of written liberties: its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356">Joyous Entry of 1356</a> bound every ruler to respect the estates' rights, and its city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> became the commercial capital of sixteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> divided the duchy.</em></p>

